## Tutorials

Normally,privatemembers of a class can only be accessed using public methods likegetters and setters. But in some cases, you can use a special function called afriendfunction to access them directly.

Afriendfunction is not a member of the class, but it is allowed to access the class's private data:

### Code Examples

```
Example
class Employee {
  private:
    int salary;

  public:
    Employee(int s) {
      salary = s;
    }

    // Declare friend function
    friend void displaySalary(Employee emp);
};

void displaySalary(Employee emp) {
  cout << "Salary: " << emp.salary;
}

int main() {
  Employee myEmp(50000);
  displaySalary(myEmp);
  return 0;
}
Try it Yourself »
```

